\title{forecastingtools: teaching-oriented tools for forecast evaluation}
\description{
R port of the Python package Forecasting-Tools (ATSE, UC3M).
\code{\link[=static_forecast]{static_forecast()}} produces sequential one-step forecasts from a model
fitted with \code{\link[forecast:Arima]{forecast::Arima()}} or \code{\link[forecast:auto.arima]{forecast::auto.arima()}}. Every other
function takes plain numeric vectors (or \code{ts} objects) of actual values and
forecasts, so it can evaluate forecasts from any source.
}
\details{
Forecast errors are always actual minus forecast. Function names and
arguments match the Python API. Tests return a list of class
\code{forecastingtools_test} with the same fields as the Python dictionaries
(\code{pvalue} is called \code{p.value}, and the coefficient estimates, standard
errors and confidence intervals are collected in one \code{coefficients} data
frame). HAC inference uses \code{\link[sandwich:NeweyWest]{sandwich::NeweyWest()}} with Bartlett weights,
no prewhitening and no small-sample adjustment, which reproduces the
Statsmodels results of the Python package.

Part of the ATSE software suite, with arimatools (ARIMA-Tools-R),
ARIMA-Tools and Forecasting-Tools (Python).
}
